#usda 1.0
(
    doc = """DEADFALL DEPOT module: materials. Owned by one specialist agent - do not edit from another module.

    Two things live in this layer.

    1. /World/Looks - the shared look library.

       94 of the 119 looks are bundled OmniPBR; 85 of those are driven by
       EXPLICIT absolute vMaterials_2 texture URLs (53 distinct maps). That is
       deliberate and it is the result of three measured findings, in the order
       they were found:

       (a) vMaterials presets cannot be made dark. Asphalt_Fine's brightness
       input spans only a 0.65-1.00 diffuse multiplier and
       Concrete_Wall_Aged_Scratched_Heavy_Grime's `diffuse_brightness` maps to
       a 0.0-0.3 blend toward black, so under this key the yard rendered as a
       pale slab and the dock face as flat cream no matter what was authored.
       OmniPBR's albedo_brightness is a true multiplier over the full range.

       (b) vMaterials presets force `infinite_tiling: true`. That routes every
       lookup through endless_texture(), a rotate-and-blend histogram trick
       that visibly destroys high-frequency detail. It is the single largest
       cause of the "airbrushed" read. OmniPBR has no such path.

       (c) THE BIG ONE - 20_architecture's box UVs are broken on every vertical
       face, and vMaterials can only read primvars:st. Measured on the composed
       stage: /World/Architecture/Dock/Slab0 is a 48.0 x 11.0 x 1.2 m box; its
       front face (the dock face that closes DETAIL_WET_APRON at 14.6 m) has st
       corners (-4.64,-4.35) (43.36,-4.35) (43.36,-3.15) (-4.64,-3.15) against
       world corners (2,-23,0) (2,-23,1.2) (-46,-23,1.2) (-46,-23,0). u runs UP
       the 1.2 m height over a 48-unit span and v runs ALONG the 48 m length
       over a 1.2-unit span: roughly 40 texture repeats per metre vertically
       and one twentieth of a repeat per metre horizontally. That is why 76 m
       of wall read as one smeared value with fine horizontal ribbing.

       The fix, applied to 72 looks, is OmniPBR `project_uvw = 1` +
       `world_or_object = 1`. That runs base::coordinate_projection(...,
       projection_type: base::projection_cubic) in WORLD space: every face is
       projected along its own dominant axis at true metre scale, so a wall
       gets (x, z) and a floor gets (x, y) and no authored UV is consulted at
       all. It costs 20_architecture nothing and fixes every box in the module
       at once. Tiling numbers in this file are unchanged because the cubic
       projection is world-metre, exactly like the primvars:st it replaces.

       (d) MEASURED, ROUND 3 - THE MAPS THEMSELVES WERE THE PROBLEM. Every
       vMaterials *_diff map for metal is a near-flat card on purpose: the
       preset composes procedural oxide, dirt and paint layers on top of it at
       render time. Fed raw into OmniPBR you get the base card and nothing
       else. Greyscale mean / std, measured on the actual bitmaps:

           iron_pitted_steel_rust_diffuse.jpg        0.182 / 0.018   FLAT
           cracked_paint_surface_bare_metal_albedo   0.333 / 0.052   FLAT
           concrete_floor_damage_diff.jpg            0.448 / 0.041   FLAT
           blued_steel_norm.jpg                      R 0.502 / 0.002  NO RELIEF
           plate_dirt.jpg                            0.648 / 0.246   strong
           mulch_diff.jpg                            0.422 / 0.108   rust-like
           aggregate_exposed_diff.jpg                0.496 / 0.152   strong
           gravel_track_ballast_norm.jpg             R 0.485 / 0.135  strong
           mulch_norm.jpg                            R 0.499 / 0.107  strong

       Those three flat albedos were driving 41 looks, including all eight
       A_Rust_* (584 rusting members), all six A_Paint_* and the whole interior
       slab set. That is the actual cause of "the mid-ground is a matte
       painting" and of "the drum is one flat colour" - not the tiling. The
       rust family now runs on mulch_diff / plate_dirt, the painted family on
       plate_dirt, and the worn concrete on aggregate_exposed_diff. Whenever a
       map is swapped, albedo_brightness is rescaled by old_mean/new_mean so
       exposure is unchanged and only the variance moves.

       GLOSS TRAPS. `reflectionroughness_texture` was pointed at packed multi
       maps whose R channel is near zero, with 0.85-0.95 influence:

           cracked_paint_surface_crackedpaint_multi  R 0.053  -> near-mirror
           metal_smudges_rough.jpg                   R 0.060  -> near-mirror
           iron_pitted_steel_roughness_no_spots      R 0.277  -> semi-gloss

       metal_smudges_rough was on every A_Paint_* and on A_Corr_2/3/5, i.e.
       every canopy column, bridge chord and trestle leg in the level was
       rendering as polished plastic. Roughness now comes from maps with a real
       R mean (generic_surface_rough 0.745, blued_steel_multi 0.760,
       mulch_multi 0.620, aggregate_exposed_rough 0.616) at 0.55-0.70
       influence over a 0.74-0.95 constant, so nothing metal can go glossy.

       CONTRAST NEEDS FREQUENCY. plate_dirt at std 0.246 reads as polished
       granite if the tile is large: 3 blotch clusters across a 0.87 m drum is
       marble, 8 is corrosion. Anything driven by plate_dirt is held at a
       0.09-0.36 m tile; mulch_diff (half the contrast) can run 0.19-0.55 m.
       Same rule broke the dock face: aggregate_exposed at a 1.7 m tile gives
       42 mm stones and reads as terrazzo, so aggregate-driven looks are tiled
       0.44-0.60 m for 11-15 mm stones.

       CORRUGATION. The library has no corrugated-iron profile map. It does
       have Paper/textures/cardboard_norm_2.jpg, which is 84 clean parallel
       ribs per tile (measured by FFT along u). Every A_Corr_*/A_CorrFade_*
       carries it in the DETAIL normal slot at an anisotropic scale that works
       out to a 76-100 mm rib pitch across and a 2.6-3.8 m tile along. World
       cubic projection puts u on the horizontal axis of a wall and v on world
       Z, so the ribs come out vertical on cladding and along the fall on the
       canopy roof without any per-prim authoring.

       NEAR-FIELD DETAIL. Every look that is ever seen inside 10 m carries a
       second high-frequency normal at a 0.030-0.128 m tile
       (`detail_normalmap_texture` + `detail_texture_scale` +
       `detail_texture_rotate`, each look on a different source map and a
       different phase) at detail_bump_factor 0.40-0.95. The previous 0.25-0.47
       m detail tile was far too coarse to survive a 1.65 m eye at 2-5 m; the
       ground had no aggregate at all. Base tiles were tightened at the same
       time: the yard asphalt runs 0.85-2.40 m instead of 1.25-3.35 m.

       DIRECTIONAL WEATHERING. Wall looks are v-elongated - base tile is 1.2x
       to 3.8x longer down the wall than across it - so grunge and blotching in
       the scan are dragged into vertical runs. On top of that there is a
       dedicated A_Streak* set (9 looks) with cutout opacity driven by
       spongy_concrete_weathered_mask_wet / _mask_dirt, for 20_architecture to
       apply to thin bands that start at a fixing, a bolt line, a gutter mouth
       or a sill. See the STREAK HAND-OFF table below.

       vMaterials MDL is still used where the surface has correct UVs of its
       own and a scan carries the whole look: gravel, ballast, mud, weeds, the
       interior slab set, cast iron, rail head, lane paint, timber, plywood,
       dirty glass, tyre rubber, cardboard, moss.

       OmniGlass and plain untextured OmniPBR are used where there is nothing
       to scan: standing water, diesel sheen, chain-link cutout, lens emitters.

       UNITS. OmniPBR `texture_scale` MULTIPLIES the uv, so every number in
       this file is 1 / metres-per-tile and each block states its tile size in
       the comment above it. vMaterials `texture_scale` DIVIDES the uv, so on
       the remaining vMaterials blocks the number IS metres-per-tile;
       Steel_Painted_Cracked halves it again internally.

    2. The cross-module material:binding overrides. 50_materials is a STRONGER
       sublayer than 20_architecture and 10_terrain, so the `over` prims below
       replace the placeholder flat-colour OmniPBR bindings those modules
       authored, without touching one vertex of their geometry.

    Uniformity is the failure mode this layer exists to beat. Every large
    architecture bucket is a SET of looks, not one look, and each mesh picks
    from that set by a CRC32 of its own prim name - so 189 canopy roof panels
    read as a patchwork of nine weathering states and 584 rusting steel members
    as eight. Variants now differ in base tile, v-elongation, detail-normal
    SOURCE, detail tile, detail phase, albedo, tint, metallic and roughness -
    not just in one number - because when they only differed in inputs that a
    vMaterials preset ignored, they rendered identical.

    ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    THIS FILE IS GENERATED. Source: tools/_mat_base.usda + tools/_mat_tune.py.
    Edit the base, then `cd tools && uv run _mat_tune.py --ship`. Hand-patching
    50_materials.usda is discarded by the next run.

    _mat_tune.py owns four numbers on every look - base tile, detail tile,
    normal strength, albedo contrast - because they are the whole of the
    `detail_density` problem and there are 134 looks to keep consistent.

    (e) MEASURED, ROUND 4 - THE DENOISER IS THE LOW-PASS FILTER, NOT THE MAPS.
    90_cameras authors `omni:rtx:pt:denoising:enabled = 1` on every
    RenderProduct. Forced to 0 at 512 spp, LANE_EYE_YARD measures
    detail_density 0.368 and DETAIL_WET_APRON 0.667; with it on, the identical
    frames measure 0.044 and 0.023. Sweeps against one fixed scene state:

        base tile x2 / x4 / x8             +6% / +15% / +40%
        base tile /2                       -5%
        detail_texture_scale clamped 8-12  +-0.001   (nothing)
        detail_texture_scale x4, x20       +-0.001   (nothing)
        detail_bump_factor x2              +-0.002   (nothing)
        bump_factor x2                     +-0.002   (nothing)
        geometry_normal_roughness_str 0    +-0.001   (nothing)
        ao_to_diffuse 1.0                  +-0.002   (nothing)
        albedo contrast x1.5/2/3/5         +13% / +25% / +45% / +80%

    The denoiser is guided by the albedo and normal AOVs, so only ALBEDO
    signal reaches the frame. Every normal map, roughness map and detail
    normal in this file is doing real work for the *look* and none for the
    metric. That is why round 4 moved the two things that change albedo as
    seen on screen: tile size (a tile whose texel footprint is far below one
    screen pixel mips to its own mean, which is exactly the "one flat value"
    failure) and contrast.

    (f) TILE SIZE IS A SAMPLING PROBLEM. Every map here is 4096^2 (cardboard
    and a few others 2048^2). Std surviving a k-texel box average - i.e. what
    is left after mipping to a k-texel screen footprint:

        k =                     1      2      4      8     16     32     64
        asphalt_fine_tarred   0.140  0.125  0.098  0.065  0.038  0.023  0.015
        aggregate_exposed     0.153  0.151  0.145  0.136  0.122  0.099  0.065
        mulch_diff            0.113  0.104  0.091  0.075  0.058  0.042  0.029
        plate_dirt            0.246  0.227  0.204  0.180  0.155  0.134  0.118
        concrete_wall_aged    0.049  0.046  0.042  0.039  0.036  0.034  0.032

    A 1920-wide 44-66 deg frame is ~4e-4 rad/pixel, so ground at 5-15 m is
    2-6 mm per pixel. The previous 1.3 m asphalt tile is a 13-texel footprint
    and keeps 0.048 of its 0.140 std - three quarters of the map gone before
    the denoiser sees it. Tiles are now 2-3x larger per class (ground 0.30,
    cladding/steel 0.35, timber/dressing 0.55, small parts 0.70), which is as
    far as they go before the depicted aggregate reads oversized.

    (g) DRUMS ARE PINNED, NOT MULTIPLIED. steeldrum_a01 is 0.64 x 0.66 x
    0.87 m. Round 3 put the D_* family on a 0.10-0.12 m tile, which at 12 m is
    0.03 mm per texel against a 7 mm pixel: 200:1, so it mips to its own mean
    and the drum is one flat colour. The whole D_* family is now pinned to an
    absolute 0.30 m tile (texture_scale 3.33) - three repeats across the body,
    so the rolling hoops, the lid ring and the pitting are separate reads. A
    class multiplier cannot do this: the multiplier that rescues 100 m of yard
    would put a fifth of one repeat on a drum.

    (h) CONTRAST IS CAPPED BY PHYSICS, NOT BY TASTE. The stretch is
    brightness *= k, add -= mean*brightness*(k-1), which holds the mean and
    multiplies the variance. Uncapped at k=2.8 it measured *worse*:
    DETAIL_WET_APRON mean_luma 0.259 -> 0.112 and firefly_frac 0.0020 ->
    0.0119, because a large negative `add` is not "very dark" - MDL hands the
    negative value to diffuse_reflection_bsdf as a tint and the path tracer
    clamps it per bounce, losing light and gaining variance. k is now capped
    three ways per look: the map's -2 sigma texel must not go negative,
    albedo_add must not fall below -0.055, and the brightest texel must stay
    under 0.90 albedo (over 1.0 is an energy-gaining surface and fireflies).
    In practice that lands k near 1.2 on every map, and exposure is held.

    (i) ROUND 5 - THE SHADER HAS TWO ALBEDO SLOTS AND THIS LIBRARY WAS USING
    ONE. Rounds 1-4 all tried to make one scan carry a whole surface, and all
    three of the critic's "flat plate" findings survived because one scan
    cannot. Reading the shipped MDL (ovrtx/bin/library/mdl/Base/
    OmniPBR_ClearCoat.mdl:610-637) settles it: OmniPBR composes

        base_color = lerp(tint*diffuse, tint*diffuse*AO, ao_to_diffuse)

    so `ao_texture` is a second, freely-chosen ALBEDO layer. It is sampled
    with `transformed_uvw`, the same transform as the diffuse map, so it
    cannot carry its own tile - but it can carry a different, uncorrelated
    map, and that is the whole win. 99 of the 134 looks now run one.

    MEASURED, on the failing surface. The critic's flat plate is
    /World/Architecture/Dock/SlabFront3 (X -4.2..+2, Y -22, Z 0..1.2, bound
    to A_Concrete_2), 15.1 m from the DETAIL_WET_APRON lens. Modelling the
    shader arithmetic on the actual 4096^2 bitmaps, box-averaging to the
    frame's real texel footprint and converting to sRGB at the exposure the
    delivered frame implies:

        spongy_concrete_weatheredMoss alone      p95-mean  0.031   <- round 4
        x spongy_concrete_dirt_mask   w=1.0                0.061
        x plate_dirt                  w=1.0                0.065
        x plate_dirt + contrast k=1.8                      0.079

    i.e. the round-4 dock face carried 0.031 of albedo spread and the 0.081
    the critic measured on it was almost all path-trace speckle. One scan
    cannot reach 0.15 at this exposure by any tiling - the maps do not have
    the range - so the second layer plus a grime-driven roughness is the fix,
    and the honest statement of the limit is in the report.

    (j) THE AO LOOKUP IS A COLOUR, NOT A MASK. `ao_color = ao_lookup.tint`.
    The first round-5 render put mulch_diff - mean RGB (0.569, 0.404, 0.293) -
    over a fifth of the level at weight 0.8 and turned the whole frame sepia:
    DETAIL_WET_APRON went cool_pixel_frac 0.533 -> 0.183 in one step. Both
    the LEVEL and the HUE are therefore compensated in _mat_tune.py:
    albedo_brightness/add are divided by the AO's luminance mean, and
    diffuse_tint is divided per channel by the AO's mean colour normalised on
    that luminance. The layer contributes its variance - including its hue
    variance, which is what real grime looks like - and none of its mean.

    (k) WHY NOT THE CRITIC'S 0.15-0.35 m TILE. Every map here is 4096^2, so a
    T-metre tile has a T/4096 m texel; the 45 mm lens at 15 m is 6.0 mm per
    pixel, so the mip footprint is k = 24.6/T texels. T = 0.30 m is k = 82,
    where spongy_concrete keeps 0.057 of its 0.130 sigma and concrete_wall
    keeps 0.032 of 0.049 - the face gets FLATTER, which is the failure being
    reported. T = 1.0-1.4 m is k = 18-25, where spongy_concrete_dirt_mask
    still keeps 0.115 of its 0.132. The wall concrete tile therefore moves to
    ~1.0 m across / ~1.35 m down - one full weathering cycle over the 1.2 m
    dock face - and the DETAIL NORMAL, which costs nothing at the mip, is
    taken to the 0.05 m the critic asked for so the same looks read correctly
    on the jersey barriers and kerbs that are seen at 2 m.

    (l) THE ABSOLUTE TILE PIN HAD DELETED THE DRUM SET. Pinning a class to an
    absolute metres-per-tile normalises the geometric mean of texture_scale,
    which meant all fifteen D_* looks came out of round 4 with byte-identical
    `texture_scale = (8.31579, 3.33111)`: every drum in the level carried the
    same corrosion at the same frequency, which is the repetition the brief
    bans outright. The pin stays - a 0.87 m drum and 100 m of yard cannot
    share a multiplier - but the tile and its v-elongation are now jittered
    per look off the look's own name, and each look draws a different second
    albedo layer, so no two drums repeat.

    (m) THE WATER WAS BEING WARMED. W_DieselSheen and W_OilyWater had no
    class of their own and fell through to DEFAULT_CLASS, whose deblue is
    0.80 - the level was mirroring the cool out of the diesel sheen and the
    bund water. Standing water is the cool half of every wet-yard frame and
    is exactly the pixel population `cool_pixel_frac` counts, so W_* now has
    its own class with deblue off. G_Interior's deblue also goes to zero:
    INTERIOR_AISLE measures cool_pixel_frac 0.058 against a 0.25 floor and
    warming the slab albedo under a sodium key is what took the last of the
    cool away."""
    metersPerUnit = 1
    upAxis = "Z"
)

# =========================================================================
# CANONICAL BINDING TABLE  -  /World/Looks/<name> -> the slots that use it
# =========================================================================
# This layer does not just publish looks, it BINDS them. 50_materials is a
# stronger sublayer than 40_vegetation, 30_props, 20_architecture and
# 10_terrain, so the `over` prims in the MATERIAL BINDINGS section below
# replace the flat placeholder OmniPBR bindings those modules author,
# without touching one vertex of their geometry. Verified on the composed
# stage: 3031 of 3031 meshes resolve to a /World/Looks material.
#
# Owners of the other modules: do not re-point these. If you add geometry,
# add its prim to the binding section here (or tell this module's owner the
# prim path) rather than authoring a local material.
#
# GROUND  (10_terrain)
#   G_AsphaltOxidised        Terrain/Ground/Ground_CentralYard        yard base, 2.20 m tile
#   G_AsphaltOxidised_B      Terrain/Ground/Ground_FlankEast          1.45 m tile, rot 37
#   G_AsphaltOxidised_C      Ground_CentralYard/sub_AsphaltCracked    1.85 m tile, rot 118
#   G_AsphaltOxidised_D      Terrain/Ground/Ground_FlankWest          2.65 m tile, rot 214
#   G_AsphaltOxidised_E      Ground_FlankEast/sub_AsphaltCracked      3.35 m tile, rot 301
#   G_AsphaltPatchOxide      Terrain/Patches/GroundPatches            2.10 m tile, rot 82
#   G_AsphaltPatchTar        GroundPatches/sub_AsphaltFresh           1.25 m tile, rot 249
#   G_AsphaltFresh           GroundPatches (fresh black band)         1.65 m tile, rot 66
#   G_AsphaltCracked         Ground_FlankWest/sub_AsphaltCracked      3.00 m tile, rot 155
#   G_AsphaltAggregate       GroundPatches/sub_AsphaltAggregate       0.85 m tile
#   G_ConcreteDock           Terrain/Ground/Ground_DockApron          dock apron slab
#   G_ConcreteExposed        Ground_CentralYard (older slab showing)
#   G_ConcreteAlgae          Terrain/Kerbs/DockFootAlgaeBand, Architecture/Dock
#   G_ConcreteKerb           Terrain/Kerbs/*
#   G_Gravel  G_Ballast      Ground margins, RailSpur ballast bed
#   G_Mud  G_MudWet          Ground_ServiceRoad, ruts, fuel-bay overflow
#   G_DirtWeeds              all four out-of-bounds margins
#   G_OilPad                 Terrain/Patches (tanker gantry pad)
#   G_BrokenConcrete         Terrain/Debris/BrokenSlabs
#   G_PuddleBed  G_DampRing  Terrain/Patches/PuddleMargins, WetWheelTracks
#   G_InteriorWorn/Wet/Oil/Scuff/Joint   Terrain/InteriorOverlay/*
#   G_SandDrift              east fence-line drift
#   W_StandingWater(Calm/Silt), W_DieselSheen, W_OilyWater
#                            Terrain/Water/P1..P6, W01..W20, ditch, bund sump
#   M_CastIron/Worn, M_RailHead     Terrain/Drainage, Terrain/Covers, RailSpur
#   P_LineYellow  P_LineWhite       Terrain/Markings/YardPaint
#   X_Void                   drain throats and gully interiors
#
# ARCHITECTURE  (20_architecture)  - replaces its Arch_* placeholders:
#   Arch_concrete        -> A_Concrete_0..4      Warehouse plinths, Dock, FuelBund
#   Arch_concrete_wet    -> A_ConcreteWet_0..1   Dock face, permanently wet bands
#   Arch_concrete_block  -> A_Block_0..2         DockOffice, GuardHut blockwork
#   (precast)            -> A_Precast_0..2       ServiceRoadWall panels
#   (barriers)           -> A_Jersey_0..2        JerseyBarriers, centre cluster
#   Arch_steel_painted   -> A_Paint_0..5         ConveyorBridge, DockCanopy,
#                                                PipeTrestle, Mezzanine steel
#   Arch_steel_rust      -> A_Rust_0..7          WaterTower, Silos, GantryCrane,
#                                                PipeTrestle, EastPlatform
#   Arch_steel_galv      -> A_Galv_0..4          PerimeterFence, handrails
#   Arch_corrugated      -> A_Corr_0..5          DockCanopy roof + ConveyorBridge
#                                                skin + Warehouse cladding
#   Arch_corrugated_rust -> A_Corr_0/1/2/4       heavy-oxide subset of the above
#   Arch_corrugated_faded-> A_CorrFade_0..2      chalked-out cladding
#   Arch_glass_dirty     -> A_Glass_0..2         Warehouse glazing, office windows
#   Arch_chainlink       -> A_Chainlink_0..1     PerimeterFence mesh (cutout)
#   Arch_plywood         -> A_Ply_0..1           boarded openings
#   Arch_rubber          -> A_Rubber_0..1        dock bumpers
#   (timber)             -> A_Timber_0..3        RailSpur sleepers, pallets
#   (chequer plate)      -> M_ChequerPlate       every walkway, stair and catwalk
#   (silo skin)          -> A_Silo_0..1          Silos, WaterTower tank
#   (practical lenses)   -> FX_LensSodium / FX_LensMercury / FX_LensDead
#
# PROPS / VEGETATION  (30_props, 40_vegetation) - offered, and consumed:
#   S_Cardboard  S_ShrinkWrap  S_Tarp  S_TyreRubber  S_DrumSteel*  S_MossPatch
#   plus A_Jersey_*, A_Timber_*, A_Ply_*, A_Concrete_2/3, A_Precast_1
#
# =========================================================================
# STREAK HAND-OFF  ->  FOR 20_architecture
# =========================================================================
# Nine new looks author weathering as MATERIAL, not as painted geometry. Each
# is a cutout-opacity look: the alpha comes from a real run/wash mask stretched
# hard in v, so the stain is shaped like gravity. They are meant to be applied
# to THIN VERTICAL BANDS OF GEOMETRY THAT START AT A FIXING AND RUN DOWN - a
# bolt line, a gutter mouth, a sill, a hatch, a flange, a cut end. A band that
# starts in mid-air reads as a stripe; a band that starts at a fixing reads as
# a stain. Suggested band widths in brackets.
#
#   A_StreakRust_0   [0.10-0.25 m]  narrow saturated bleed straight off a bolt,
#                                   fixing washer, cleat or cut end. The one to
#                                   use most - 3-6 per elevation, never evenly
#                                   spaced. Warehouse cladding fixings, canopy
#                                   purlin cleats, conveyor-bridge skin seams.
#   A_StreakRust_1   [0.30-0.60 m]  older, wider, faded at the edges. Under a
#                                   corroded gutter bracket or a hinge.
#   A_StreakRust_2   [0.8-2.0 m]    broad orange bloom, not a run. Whole panel
#                                   below a rotted gutter; silo hatch aprons.
#   A_StreakWater_0  [0.15-0.40 m]  cool grey-green run off a sill or a coping.
#                                   Dock-office window heads, parapet drips.
#   A_StreakWater_1  [1.5-4.0 m]    broad soft grime wash over a whole panel.
#                                   Dock face between bays; blockwork elevations.
#   A_StreakEfflor_0 [0.10-0.30 m]  pale calcium bloom creeping out of a wet
#                                   joint. Dock face construction joints, bund
#                                   wall lifts, precast panel joints.
#   A_StreakAlgae_0  [0.4-1.2 m]    green-black film. North faces, the bottom
#                                   400 mm of anything, the dock foot.
#   A_StreakOil_0    [0.15-0.50 m]  near-black gloss seep from a flange, a
#                                   gearbox, a drum that has been leaking for a
#                                   decade. Reads wet when nothing else does.
#   A_StreakScuff_0  [0.6-2.5 m]    HORIZONTAL grain, not vertical. Lay it along
#                                   a wheel path or a forklift line on the deck
#                                   and the dock nosing. Use this instead of a
#                                   stamped ring decal.
#
# All nine are world cubic-projected, so they work on a band of any orientation
# without needing correct UVs - see the UV note below.
#
# =========================================================================
# HOW THE VARIANT SETS WORK
#   Every large bucket is a SET, never one look. Each mesh picks from its set
#   by a CRC32 of its own prim name, so 189 canopy roof panels read as nine
#   weathering states and 584 rusting members as eight. Variants differ in base
#   tile, v-elongation, detail-normal SOURCE, detail tile, detail phase, albedo,
#   tint, metallic AND roughness. They used to differ only in inputs that a
#   vMaterials preset silently ignored, which is why they rendered identical.
#
# UNITS AND UVs  -  READ THIS BEFORE CHANGING A TILING NUMBER
#   72 looks are authored with OmniPBR project_uvw = 1, world_or_object = 1,
#   i.e. base::projection_cubic in WORLD space. primvars:st is NOT consulted on
#   those looks. This is not a stylistic choice: 20_architecture's box UVs are
#   broken on every vertical face. Measured, /World/Architecture/Dock/Slab0
#   (48.0 x 11.0 x 1.2 m) maps u up its 1.2 m HEIGHT over a 48-unit span and v
#   along its 48 m LENGTH over a 1.2-unit span - about 40 repeats per metre
#   vertically, one twentieth of a repeat per metre horizontally. That front
#   face is the dock face in DETAIL_WET_APRON. Cubic world projection makes it
#   correct without touching one vertex, and every tiling number below stays a
#   real-world metre size because the projection is world-metre.
#   - OmniPBR `texture_scale` multiplies the uv, so it is authored as
#     1 / metres-per-tile. Every OmniPBR block below notes its tile size.
#     texture_scale = (0.95238, 0.75758) means 1.05 m across, 1.32 m down.
#   - The remaining vMaterials looks still read primvars:st, and there
#     `texture_scale` DIVIDES the uv, so the number IS metres-per-tile.
#     Steel_Painted_Cracked halves it again internally.
#   20_architecture: if you ever fix the box UV generator, nothing here breaks -
#   cubic projection simply keeps winning. If you want a look to follow authored
#   UVs instead, tell this module's owner and project_uvw comes off that look.
# =========================================================================

over "World"
{
    def Scope "Looks" (
        doc = "Shared look library for DEADFALL DEPOT. Bind from any module."
    )
    {
        # Dominant yard surface: grey oxidised asphalt with micro-puddles in the low
        # spots, so roughness varies across the surface rather than being one
        # number.
        # YARD BASE. Oxidised grey-black asphalt, 2.20 m per tile. Dominant
        # surface of Lane B and the single most-scrutinised material in the map.
        def Material "G_AsphaltOxidised"
        {
            token outputs:mdl:surface.connect = </World/Looks/G_AsphaltOxidised/Shader.outputs:out>
            token outputs:surface.connect = </World/Looks/G_AsphaltOxidised/Preview.outputs:surface>

            def Shader "Shader"
            {
                uniform token info:implementationSource = "sourceAsset"
                uniform asset info:mdl:sourceAsset = @OmniPBR.mdl@
                uniform token info:mdl:sourceAsset:subIdentifier = "OmniPBR"
                asset inputs:diffuse_texture = @https://omniverse-content-production.s3.us-west-2.amazonaws.com/Materials/vMaterials_2/Ground/textures/asphalt_fine_tarred_diff.jpg@
                asset inputs:ao_texture = @https://omniverse-content-production.s3.us-west-2.amazonaws.com/Materials/vMaterials_2/Ground/textures/aggregate_exposed_diff.jpg@
                float inputs:ao_to_diffuse = 0.600
                asset inputs:normalmap_texture = @https://omniverse-content-production.s3.us-west-2.amazonaws.com/Materials/vMaterials_2/Ground/textures/asphalt_fine_tarred_norm.jpg@
                uniform float inputs:bump_factor = 2.4500
                asset inputs:reflectionroughness_texture = @https://omniverse-content-production.s3.us-west-2.amazonaws.com/Materials/vMaterials_2/Ground/textures/asphalt_fine_tarred_multi_R_rough_G_ao_B_height.jpg@
                float inputs:reflection_roughness_texture_influence = 0.68
                float inputs:reflection_roughness_constant = 0.48
                color3f inputs:diffuse_tint = (0.830, 0.902, 1.046)
                float inputs:albedo_brightness = 0.3285
                float inputs:albedo_desaturation = 0.5
                float inputs:albedo_add = -0.0550
                float inputs:specular_level = 0.5
                asset inputs:detail_normalmap_texture = @https://omniverse-content-production.s3.us-west-2.amazonaws.com/Materials/vMaterials_2/Ground/textures/hard_court_norm.jpg@
                uniform float inputs:detail_bump_factor = 0.9000
                float2 inputs:detail_texture_scale = (12.00000, 12.00000)
                float inputs:detail_texture_rotate = 17.0
                bool inputs:project_uvw = 1
                bool inputs:world_or_object = 1
                float2 inputs:texture_scale = (0.23077, 0.23077)
                float2 inputs:texture_translate = (0.13, 0.71)
                float inputs:texture_rotate = 0.0
                token outputs:out
            }

            def Shader "Preview"
            {
                uniform token info:id = "UsdPreviewSurface"
                color3f inputs:diffuseColor = (0.062, 0.06, 0.058)
                float inputs:metallic = 0
                float inputs:roughness = 0.78
                float inputs:opacity = 1
                float inputs:specular = 0.5
                token outputs:surface
            }
        }
